#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197941 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "c8dd9065-9db8-53da-b6a5-cf6cddd3a3e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11675" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/a8d3e8c54d61326d9f7d5203d32bd48006c404e9d3990820cb8884c08df8dba3.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$ and $u_2(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{\n(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{z} \n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type I region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/efc1013a8202f8eea26250b904cf3d4672b30d478ddaad72b6347b274bd33f7d.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[3]{z}, \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = y^3.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region is $z = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{\n(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: y^3\\leq z \\leq 1\n\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/3ae61409e70d4d6d6227a306ab1651331d9b40633248fd525cefbab519202a52.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197942 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "83df52ed-139b-5dff-8b32-942aafeff0ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-11676" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x.$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(x),$ $v_2(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first draw the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{\n(x,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1\n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/8007b0f7348f9855a009aff56c676122a739e6210d4f024c524e72e3cfa23312.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0 \\leq y \\leq  1-z^2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/97d0f5fc56498e1a044dbbfc07da571c1806903822dfdb1d68f877723ce9a559.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f5fab194951b50a2a0c81aff8d422b98be5145bbef3123ea746b86bcebd1e778.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny = 1-z^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = \\sqrt{1- y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{1-y} \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/403675d42fae7e437e62bcb3e254e3f7cf3997556606ed529807d83253d1c439.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/1e1fbae22c5ad39225a5de4e3c3aeb025cb41b21bbf0c127da57e3ada10c3087.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ then $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{0}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\sqrt{1-y}}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197966 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "15684778-4ac6-5741-b17b-4ba9f8a193e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12405" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(y),$ $v_2(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n=\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/9fe61cd50d31086b47495f0bd0fd71308fc30286d392f4d0064cf743b93272b0.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $z \\leq x \\leq 2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/6af345af2d33b53c698e8e1d4eff10e3b3116911e14ee1b4581da7eee8528f94.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/53ec4e56ca38362b10c05d4cc657f9057005d9692e0fcde1f0846e7e80b4f478.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\nx = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5ce67f398f60237d763d393d62f9e7346270b954acc7efdc7a096a5f3155f4d3.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/0dbf7a35fe74730db7c2a07fc4bbe881a66412cee53bf0a606c522f5493e58c2.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8d91a9d-c94f-59b2-899c-43e061632bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379507} {:answer/id #uuid "e82becf2-4775-5741-b54e-9423249b90f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1+4x}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379508} {:answer/id #uuid "ef125ac7-dff2-5108-8226-054cce41290a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+4x}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379509} {:answer/id #uuid "8bec214f-809b-585a-8562-48908e2fde0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379510} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce389b3-476f-5b37-902b-8ee51e85a52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bec214f-809b-585a-8562-48908e2fde0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379510} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eada532-6efd-5f53-b0e2-e4af31829129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef46d483-e881-585c-a3d4-01fb7fd607a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162398" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/9f4f24dd100758466d8a06eee113bada8b1bede4b48c0a3e694e4c1e3f1c2371.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-z}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type I region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/dc1a9b6371456f635cd4b73fe99301fa349ceeec1341b9bd3b86f6bcd7ad8ba2.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=1-\\frac{x}{4}-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{4}-y.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/07c4ee04dfba186348b3bd56b182477cec9ca1a581e37bb0eb4ab9a3d64ee515.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad1db02d-bc60-5e15-9acf-f6469996ddb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(y-z)/3}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379514} {:answer/id #uuid "97b062bb-d0fc-5539-935f-15eb7f3aafbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-y+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379515} {:answer/id #uuid "947b1f2d-feae-539e-b8c0-e95131a24b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379516} {:answer/id #uuid "75b9ba23-367a-50b6-81f9-04b4e7a27e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379517} {:answer/id #uuid "c94491e0-8ed6-5685-b08f-241c27ce49e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-y+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b9ba23-367a-50b6-81f9-04b4e7a27e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379517} :answer-field/id #uuid "17307b0e-32f9-5c9e-a3cd-de96ba37d1e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bfd9cc0-6de0-5fe8-89c3-170f6830fede" :question/math-academy-id "q-162400" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/bd69636822b7a3f68805273a4ffd72c7011a4f945a3ebbac8dcd99191e7ea730.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then over a region $D$ in the $yz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{3-x}{3},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x-3y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type II region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d26a7cc2c643b300226136a223be0fdebed4e17c99c2e562d1cc209caa023ea5.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=3-x-3y\\,⟹\\,x=3-3y-z.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 3-3y-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5641e6fc83054a355a2010084e1c8be2cd2d8144eae2aad0706704247f03b258.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab5cf20f-f199-5f62-ba4d-691ddaebbeb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1-{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379521} {:answer/id #uuid "ffaf36a6-1c2c-5150-bf59-28b08fbf1555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379522} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbc553d-3c53-5d3d-bb2a-22ed0e2b451d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379523} {:answer/id #uuid "37f165cf-6bac-54bc-ad3e-32cfab5ee1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379524} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee5118e-4a44-5ea6-9a38-4c30c8c34c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}-1}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffaf36a6-1c2c-5150-bf59-28b08fbf1555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379522} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e6139a-8c58-5abe-a0f2-789deafcee91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "133c8185-f1c9-5cfa-88e2-7697817cdd72" :question/math-academy-id "q-162481" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d76d928d43054c7d5c026cc25b95fb55855c7a8cbdb5fb35b5ae1f1d39a35c90.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(x,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le (y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/116ae6061410bca820b3c89869b23a36b5cef47e4fe483e390ee43d1e52ff726.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=(y-1)^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-\\sqrt{z}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5613cb5b9718914e35f7259e3fe9dfd69ce128a861d19995e2c836c2b76d0f58.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8663d08e-227a-5b32-b410-ee4fc0676845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379528} {:answer/id #uuid "cac70e86-1bdd-5b9f-bbc8-42ab51e6ffde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-2y}{\\int }_{2y}^{1-z/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379529} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2c3b89-84a2-5c30-94d6-23d2ea7390e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-2z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379530} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcab143-c79a-53e6-9d77-b107b1359e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{1}^{1+z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379531} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4a148d-b73c-5bab-a07c-f708560b88f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8663d08e-227a-5b32-b410-ee4fc0676845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379528} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ac1447d-435b-56b8-8afe-3b5c7e418ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f03b73ae-4f63-5979-88b7-c08aa4b95288" :question/math-academy-id "q-162482" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/192cb96645cef0340e99769af3126b9d7e3775ed419e8414e3ad1ed683e919e3.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z),$ ${u}_{2}(y,z),$ ${v}_{1}(y),$ ${v}_{2}(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type II region and its projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/60f61df1520d366f6cbcac0b87e7e5eb453e8f011ff046b29a61cdae41624ddf.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the rear boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=1-x\\,⟹\\,x=1-z.\n$$\n\n- The rear boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 1-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/02bbf0de5dad7ff72970ef5a939ac66fd05dfc06c31a23c37b6204042e1859b8.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $yz$-plane with the top curve given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-z \\\\ x=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{y}{2}=1-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2eea77ae3d1ae7a459dba98c98b20fabbec6b1d3a6cacb718b32688bd4ad11d7.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13cef9f3-2cbe-55cb-8482-9d01db8496ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379535} {:answer/id #uuid "26db765d-9df2-5ab9-aabc-e7da9a149933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379536} {:answer/id #uuid "be10a8bc-8a53-50d2-ad26-415d18f394d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379537} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2de698-e1ce-5a5d-be85-d210be2ec97a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379538} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1362e0-244a-598a-a3a7-d59b8cee24b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be10a8bc-8a53-50d2-ad26-415d18f394d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379537} :answer-field/id #uuid "14340785-a496-5f6c-bc94-7885ea7befbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df891a57-61b8-59e7-aa5b-534b4f8e90c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162488" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/380b44a8baf8ed15eb3eb4e66e77b820f5bc51a73be840526db67d96f6d763ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}y}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/185f85445f95382afdd9516351f0cce3c4d2b11d99f65da2d5a36db0fc853215.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/6aeba17b071bd275be7147ffb925b488881a2fb8de5f544dbffe8caab5c036e4.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}y\\,⟹\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,2x\\le y\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/be089ef60080ad9181db8a5dacccf6abe382bef492369342fa15761134b507d2.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve given by $x=\\frac{1}{2}(2)=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/885b884fee574cae3c2f62ac0092e64b9be1731ae8510f6e910d0a101ffa3f20.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}[{\\int }_{2x}^{2}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2-2x}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2(1-x)}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[z]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54da0d3a-37ff-5da3-a7ab-ff4a86fa3803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379542} {:answer/id #uuid "67af4759-3e72-5401-95f4-301081967371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379543} {:answer/id #uuid "f4207370-0893-51ad-91d0-e2362b7e8b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379544} {:answer/id #uuid "c04e6654-b0ab-5ffa-aebd-2c79ed568e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379545} {:answer/id #uuid "664b8778-c76e-5ded-a181-80da69a0112d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67af4759-3e72-5401-95f4-301081967371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379543} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1309f76-71f8-526e-8be3-9795b9754fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51e3e7e7-44a6-54ed-a3c2-de6f58c215e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162489" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7c959b9c517b17d9427de450def510b9673d91fd951e28696d4d56d8a768b74d.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ and then $z.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/c3837a3551b35a5146fd2c8e2441e4a9d6d947e8c52e44254d48eb854e54e605.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/22581a56f4d9cb611bd9a58b06b1036cd47b500217570ae6890d0f38dba6742c.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve given by \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\sqrt{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/e2305d3da63886703666fd58a2adfe8f15f2eceb7261922f46ba4949b0fc0cde.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ then $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5485e981-ff4f-5aa9-8652-76f0566871b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379549} {:answer/id #uuid "a317900d-f4ec-5c1b-87a6-8f25bad7538d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379550} {:answer/id #uuid "6d54fa48-01d9-5098-b7ae-7d0f07714770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379551} {:answer/id #uuid "fc854e0b-7d23-514f-b8a9-fb0d67455950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379552} {:answer/id #uuid "e407edc0-7d90-51e3-a5c6-c2c3284b9cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a317900d-f4ec-5c1b-87a6-8f25bad7538d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379550} :answer-field/id #uuid "8373aa6b-ddd2-54c8-870a-18ed8ade520d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49e6f669-bf26-5998-975d-2dce4d5661ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-162500" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z),$ ${u}_{2}(y,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\frac{z}{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/29c722bce3c23c2af628f18dfaf16695f9ec5beac68b0d16ce93d6a993d6fb8c.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/cdc8fb2c7f6ef9a28b01094ef3bf18089297a6ef9a431c5152311e5e92cd2557.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type II region and its projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d805df26c5a471323d855194b41e052142e711e7f7f9ddf910d792ae74cbc2c6.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4d40e420abeec633cd145dca33071d6459e4b4009e4c6cfc871d6c6150be51e4.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $yz$-plane with the left curve given by $y=\\sqrt{0}=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/81782a6c847e5369dfed7524619de354ff7676a856685fb4340d09cf61dca339.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}{[y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\cdot [{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y] \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0)\\cdot (-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a47f237d-c9ef-518a-8d24-152bdb99afec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379556} {:answer/id #uuid "dc197835-26dc-5181-a675-84dadfaf4c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{z}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379557} {:answer/id #uuid "1d445e2e-678b-5818-8d7b-d02e0ca8d579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379558} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a6c30d-de22-5640-bab2-f2cd4fc42a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379559} {:answer/id #uuid "f38c6d0c-a848-55f3-b349-da391bebc151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d445e2e-678b-5818-8d7b-d02e0ca8d579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379558} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69b9535-37b3-5f87-b0de-86953b3ee6ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "631ca64e-ffc1-5564-82ee-d1509603bf5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162501" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7094d1527f7feb9dac54cbd90e595dcbb71c11d5c0a32d1371c3c63c54127899.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/c48de52085373361c4e4de3010ebe36c1da702f0efec45b455dff891869086f2.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1-y\\,⟹\\,y=1-z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/fd5d71a6d6509851e5131fe458b9672d59eb7ff6c07499949ae199b45f4ddae0.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1-z \\\\ y=x\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,x=1-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-x.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/12743c492a7f7883a2ecd8d3ca73138a324a53040cb8936f4794f9e8d84c8fd5.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7fb4b9b-1318-53db-965a-c14c9cb44c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-x}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379563} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e7f2cf-714a-5651-8f57-55bf89be637f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379564} {:answer/id #uuid "7227c149-34ff-5d54-9a18-09ca80a699f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379565} {:answer/id #uuid "5470c29b-692f-5041-995a-37aeab82b2b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379566} {:answer/id #uuid "083c7cf0-5d12-5125-b32d-82d669af9850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{x}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5470c29b-692f-5041-995a-37aeab82b2b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379566} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca22392-1292-5484-91e9-de580cf42d8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a353cd2-5ef5-5390-ba64-a688a6f58905" :question/math-academy-id "q-162509" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x.$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type I region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/87b2dd6f4b527f2b709f3e5ede2add7e71d87145c1a15bcc4dbf7066ee41cde1.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/1ad3b88cc426acf67beba4298eb889ce986879b1ae894d5f95ab8d8f3e7b7cf2.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b401109bb512f5ad13ccef3346fd3cf2c51eb70eced978aa995f3db597408196.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2393b79260273fa658f846ed9013156dbc280195b8d7a0e9b0aeca6cd46e545d.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x \\\\ y=\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,z=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f14c08f7d809bbb9eb83dc6f34bfd69ed93963c5dab219d7e6fb16720204ae61.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ then $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b03a5e1-3023-5f5e-8777-575db44c7214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{z+1}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379570} {:answer/id #uuid "b3efbfcf-f3ff-5a57-8033-7e36832528a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379571} {:answer/id #uuid "ed50618c-72bf-52fc-b4c2-39827e54c21b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{3}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379572} {:answer/id #uuid "4394a865-b272-59f0-8147-754452a47b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{z}^{3}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379573} {:answer/id #uuid "96b72c4b-f7e3-5c69-911d-9614d7e035b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3efbfcf-f3ff-5a57-8033-7e36832528a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379571} :answer-field/id #uuid "21dfee99-4f2c-57af-b8d9-c72c538620a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "329d8805-98e9-5d1c-a1c4-99b5a4d90005" :question/math-academy-id "q-162513" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the following integral, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ then $x.$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,{y}^{3}\\le z\\le 2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type I region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/58a9056015b3dad91ec7ae64bff893c8c6c2cce6480c979545e423cbe105ef03.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${y}^{3}\\le z\\le 2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2fdd6a15b4ddadfc3ee24af5e35e2a87e9e963ceeefbf858b704d78ee0b78513.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/65da730d32f200b8f9da004ee8cf6b7e79095e323961f4a5b6dfa0bddeace42b.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz={y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt[3]{z}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/1743f540a85fc1c266c84da8f34b2012516b7bbc98d113cf7c62ba3bba8173c1.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by \n\n$$\nz=(\\sqrt[3]{2})^{3}=2.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b76bf84e1179bec6a1e8e357c6cc628eee95c1663e6f0ff375135612a6f275de.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9252664a-368a-5535-a4d8-8a4230152a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379577} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecb6f66-cb09-5883-9954-b87f1a7e6c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379578} {:answer/id #uuid "62e057a2-37d8-5846-ac4e-6b00b0a26138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379579} {:answer/id #uuid "f31248c2-4e16-57dd-8ed0-d082a94e222d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379580} {:answer/id #uuid "a83e48ff-f888-5b85-be17-19b2cb627df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f31248c2-4e16-57dd-8ed0-d082a94e222d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379580} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9bcaeb-4b04-5e34-aebd-d30064ec5e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "254fbc86-e0b9-5007-8b84-f491c3555b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163222" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/63118a7b3d7fd589513f41ae1e721baf25305f480e683c984088fedb9a8d759b.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then over a region $D$ in the $yz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,\\,:\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type II region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7dbabd349afb41c00c447ab61e28139cc7818a74c54bd17a61eb77d05ae558ae.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,\\,:\\,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4040e2d2eadc5c39b9224b1b39980de486fe6dd69dad1e63179c6cffcb4046b4.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dff45150-2ba4-5086-939f-09baf7641382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379583} {:answer/id #uuid "5e866a3f-2ce6-52d9-89ff-c7a6f8deba26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2-z}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379584} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b85c41-66b6-5b13-bffb-fc8574503bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379585} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6431ac-5b19-5e26-90fb-6f94a71a212a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379586} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eb66b8-3996-5a66-89a4-23aee607d73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2-x-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0b85c41-66b6-5b13-bffb-fc8574503bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379585} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ee165f-a8e3-5044-9cca-1c15e3ffeaf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28fccb5e-7a4d-52e5-a38d-41ce9d967777" :question/math-academy-id "q-38980" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/96b24f85c19f388c152dae265c3794179ebc9e1e6fc06a1fe10f82627b73d29c.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(x,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x-2y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f08e4fc4d1d2933e9c12fe7df96a1029d66a83c68beff4fa31928cc010554766.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=2-x-2y\\,⟹\\,y=(2-x-z)/2.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x-z}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2639dbff9d1d4269abb59ccd3eefa0b246c8d1f183fd8f6ef132a41cc07e345d.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eda6ae21-370a-5048-84c3-7f7739e3cc66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379590} {:answer/id #uuid "cc27fd6c-7f8a-5a73-8c88-a26a9979b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379591} {:answer/id #uuid "228c78bb-04fd-52a9-8dfc-ff3b1652a143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379592} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee0c284-7fb5-5caf-8f3f-03a83ab7735f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379593} {:answer/id #uuid "27c561a3-2f99-5bb7-89b8-4e62e0df1a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27c561a3-2f99-5bb7-89b8-4e62e0df1a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379594} :answer-field/id #uuid "993c9132-301f-500f-92e3-f70e8476fc80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c570f574-3af8-5e51-88f9-850621146869" :question/math-academy-id "q-38982" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/446e61491ae43e610dc39e4b46b752668969540f7733bf7d9c945fa260210a77.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b486ea542dc330a483fa76f333ab80c9fcd07434db5dc0c5a0a62a335294f127.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b626c20c336bc81df9194f161998f7c6aea524a11495fede615cdf76f5d93f59.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=y\\,⟹\\,y=z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7386453aebec047469c829e2c2c5d6c4ab915ac965bbcd39838dc7be50941d54.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the bottom curve given by $z=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4b2bb3583b5c0807c653f503cdf9ed954f38db8a30eef548738e96cedb889288.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{3}{(1-{z}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0d5e63e-20b0-51be-828c-9acb43bf5635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2} - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197612} {:answer/id #uuid "fe047bf5-e386-5cb6-b074-a4af7209cc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197613} {:answer/id #uuid "037e947e-5b63-53b6-a270-f8fc01b73549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197614} {:answer/id #uuid "52012d86-6782-596d-bdaf-cff1ea568658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197615} {:answer/id #uuid "6476b2b8-3814-5397-8637-9d2afc0455b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe047bf5-e386-5cb6-b074-a4af7209cc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197613} :answer-field/id #uuid "400e8c38-4fa2-516e-a75c-eec6573ce5a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf062656-4e4e-5cbe-a3a9-68966303708b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39154" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/8ac5fd57665f292d8a013f47aaf20226944315bb1f74695e895dca40332b28c6.png)\n\nEvaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\nThe region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/30e43d707479d17f45fb74c023d16706ba9308ffc4421cb17a23d7c2ef16fbee.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4429082d4ab0d6fdef75d79b1db02944f0c53741fb0ccf19d8a4ef46a253698f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/a941e1bfabb21561b622080775c3c5389a86cf4150f1fb28dd692a022f607620.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by $z=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/20e339260b0947f92d8fedfa47e899ed53753e03e30c4b816edec06d092d9a01.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}{[y]}_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{3}}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{e-1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b66340fc-0448-509f-bdd0-70f63048085f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197618} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5f6c7-7d42-5233-86ff-47c9bf6a1e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197619} {:answer/id #uuid "f05e8bff-5afb-53ae-b0e8-eb875051bfd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197620} {:answer/id #uuid "fefc612b-9c21-5a5c-a197-315432ecde3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197621} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4f91c9-d66d-5c27-954f-9c5de8bcede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5f6c7-7d42-5233-86ff-47c9bf6a1e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197619} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58ad5aa-d3ac-54dc-b361-02ddd2c3f413" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b2d988e7-dd3f-5642-ad43-14d09def5c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-39415" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,y),$ ${u}_{2}(x,y),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi },\\,\\,{z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/3bb477b93fb92174307bee87130d9957b135cdafaa4167f78f3d603c4192391d.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/cf966e9ec5fbcf11727d46bb3906f05e4b30df4c9bef86a6eab4850b967d7ba0.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/88b5e19c3e263d160db8d041fd3504b25d4a8a8f7ae8e111cdeb5b153c3cd21f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny={z}^{2}\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b748578888fdda878b0d44bb036310644c3437c1922aafd49cab6c165a9cf132.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=\\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/60e35d0e77314aaecc60b0eda3c039afa0bdd44d49f68ad39e27c8856a9390e0.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
